STEP 5 / DoD 4 — LIVE DRIVE: **IT DID NOT HAPPEN IN THIS SESSION.** Stated plainly, as step 5 authorises, rather than reported as a drive that did not occur. What follows is what I DID measure on the live lane, read-only, plus exactly what blocks the drive.

STORE PATH: `D:/projexts/UnAI/.moe-next/store.sqlite` (2,109,440 bytes, mtime 2026-09-05 03:59). Read READ-ONLY with `node:sqlite` `new DatabaseSync(path, { readOnly: true })`. Nothing was written to the live store by this row. Probe source is inline below so a grader can recreate it (global rail 12b).

MEASURED STATE OF THE LIVE LANE (407 domain events, 334 command decisions):
  GoalCreated: 5          GRAPH_BODY_RECORDED: 3      GoalExecutionEnabled: 3
  PlanProposed: 3         PlanningSubmissionFinalized: 3
  ReviewOutputAccepted: 3 VerifierReceiptRecorded: 3  LandingRefused: 3
  ProductContractGate1Approved: 1                     LandingBaselineRecorded: 1

THE HEADLINE MEASUREMENT, and it is the reason this row exists. **Every sealed graph on the live lane is SINGLE-NODE, and each sits on its OWN goal** — decoded from the three `GRAPH_BODY_RECORDED` payloads:
    graph-body:unai:e3d70cba…  completionNode=node-uai-core-identities        nodes(1) edges(0)
    graph-body:unai:4fd9de25…  completionNode=node-uai-evidence-ledger        nodes(1) edges(0)
    graph-body:unai:3bd6f0cc…  completionNode=evidence-ledger-durable-ingest  nodes(1) edges(0)
Three goals, three graphs, one node each, ZERO edges. That is exactly the retired world the parent is closing — "a goal is built one node at a time and growth goes through a successor goal" — still standing on the live store as of 2026-09-05. No multi-node graph has ever been sealed on UnAI. The offline e2e this row landed is therefore the FIRST place a 3-node graph with a hard chain has been built to completion on one goal anywhere in this repo.

LANDING SHAS: THERE ARE NONE. All three landings REFUSED, `sha: null`, verbatim payloads:
    landing:node-uai-core-identities        {"outcome":"REFUSED","receiptId":"363e6267…","sha":null,"subjectRef":"node-uai-core-identities"}
    landing:node-uai-evidence-ledger        {"outcome":"REFUSED","receiptId":"1d29cc8e…","sha":null,"subjectRef":"node-uai-evidence-ledger"}
    landing:evidence-ledger-durable-ingest  {"outcome":"REFUSED","receiptId":"cceef3af…","sha":null,"subjectRef":"evidence-ledger-durable-ingest"}
The `LandingRefused` event payload carries NO refusal code or detail — only outcome/receiptId/sha/subjectRef — so the live store cannot say WHY it refused. That is itself a finding: a refusal that records no reason cannot be triaged from durable state, and epic rail 4 wants stable codes on every fail-closed path. Filed as a follow-up below, not patched here (task rail 1).

NO SEAT IS WEDGED. The three newest `WorkClaimed` payloads all carry `expiresAt` in the PAST (2026-09-04T22:54:21Z, 22:54:06Z, 23:13:06Z), so no claim is live and nothing was waiting on a 30-minute TTL. The wrapper was NOT restarted (task rail 4) — there was nothing to unstick.

WHY THE DRIVE DID NOT RUN, precisely:
1. DoD 4 requires the approval to happen IN THE BROWSER. The browser's authority comes from the pairing handshake, whose second step is a HUMAN typing the daemon's confirmation label into the daemon's stdin (`mem:control-room-live-lane-recipe`: the daemon prints no prompt, a human reads the label off the page and types it back). This is a one-shot agent session with no human at the keyboard, and the lane's `lane.mjs`/`shoot.mjs` operator pipe lives in another session's scratchpad.
2. A real planning seat needs a working provider credential. Per `mem:real-codex-limit-available-on-this-host` the codex CLI is deliberately AT ITS USAGE LIMIT on this host until 09-08.
3. Landing on UnAI has refused 3/3 historically with no recorded reason, so "at least two nodes landed" was not reachable even if 1 and 2 were solved.
I did not work around any of these by substituting a scripted approval for the browser: that would make the row's headline claim false in exactly the way DoD 4 exists to prevent.

WHAT THE README REWRITE (step 6) WILL AND WILL NOT SAY: it will state the sealed-DAG/parallel-nodes/HARD-dependency truth the offline e2e proves, and it will NOT imply the live drive happened. That section's whole purpose is honesty about gaps.

PROBE SOURCE (recreate and re-run):
    import { DatabaseSync } from "node:sqlite";
    const db = new DatabaseSync("D:/projexts/UnAI/.moe-next/store.sqlite", { readOnly: true });
    const dec = new TextDecoder();
    for (const r of db.prepare("select event_type, count(*) c from domain_events group by event_type order by c desc").all()) console.log(r.event_type, r.c);
    for (const r of db.prepare("select aggregate_id, payload from domain_events where event_type='GRAPH_BODY_RECORDED' order by rowid").all()) {
      const c = JSON.parse(dec.decode(r.payload)).content ?? {};
      console.log(r.aggregate_id, c.completionNode, (c.snapshot?.nodes ?? []).length, (c.snapshot?.edges ?? []).length);
    }
    db.close();

FOLLOW-UPS for whoever picks the live lane back up (comment, not new rows — global rail 21/16):
  F1. `LandingRefused` records no refusal code or layer. Three live refusals cannot be diagnosed from the store.
  F2. The live lane's browser approval has no non-human path, so no agent session can ever discharge a "approved in the BROWSER" DoD clause unaided. A scripted operator pipe (the `lane.mjs` pattern) needs to become a committed lane script, not scratchpad.
  F3. UnAI carries 5 goals for what is one product; once multi-node lands there, the successor-goal-per-node pattern in that store is worth a cleanup pass.
